p2_say = [
    "While the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem guarantees that the maximum flow equals the minimum cut capacity, systems architects require an efficient, deterministic algorithm to extract the exact vertices and edges that constitute the minimum cut. Remarkably, once any maximum flow algorithm—such as the Edmonds-Karp algorithm—terminates upon reaching a state where no further augmenting paths exist, the minimum cut can be extracted in linear time O(V + E) directly from the final Residual Graph G_f without solving a separate combinatorial optimization problem.",
    "The extraction algorithm relies on the concept of Residual Reachability. In the final residual graph G_f produced when maximum flow is reached, there are no remaining paths from the source s to the sink t with positive residual capacity. To extract the minimum cut partition (S, T), the algorithm initializes a standard Breadth-First Search or Depth-First Search traversal rooted at the source vertex s. During this traversal, the search explores only directed residual edges (u, v) that possess strictly positive residual capacity, meaning c_f(u, v) = c(u, v) - f(u, v) > 0. The set S is defined precisely as the set of all vertices reachable from the source s in this residual traversal. The complement set T is defined as all remaining vertices in the graph: T = V \\ S.",
    "By construction, the sink vertex t cannot belong to S; if t were reachable from s via edges with positive residual capacity, an augmenting path would exist, violating the premise that f is a maximum flow. Therefore, s belongs to S and t belongs to T, certifying that (S, T) is a valid s-t cut partition. Furthermore, consider any original directed edge (u, v) in the network where u in S and v in T. If this edge had any remaining residual capacity (c(u, v) - f(u, v) > 0), vertex v would have been explored and included in S during the traversal, contradicting the fact that v belongs to T. Consequently, every forward edge crossing from S to T must be completely saturated: f(u, v) = c(u, v).",
    "Similarly, for any original edge (v, u) directed from T to S, if that edge carried any positive flow (f(v, u) > 0), the residual graph would contain a backward edge (u, v) with residual capacity c_f(u, v) = f(v, u) > 0, which would allow the search from u in S to reach v in T, again causing a contradiction. Therefore, every backward edge from T to S must carry exactly zero flow: f(v, u) = 0. Substituting these two boundary conditions into the net flow equation yields the exact identity: net flow from S to T equals sum{ c(u, v) : u in S, v in T } = c(S, T). Because net flow equals the total flow |f|, this proves constructively that c(S, T) equals the maximum flow value, certifying (S, T) as an authentic Minimum Cut.",
    "Extracting the physical minimum cut edges simply requires iterating over all edges in the original network and filtering for those where the originating node belongs to S and the destination node belongs to T. These edges represent the physical bottlenecks of the entire infrastructure. In cloud data center engineering, these saturated crossing edges are designated as Critical Chokepoints. When link congestion alerts fire in network operations centers, automated monitoring daemons execute this residual reachability sweep in sub-millisecond time, instantly pinpointing the exact fiber optic interfaces causing global traffic delays.",
    "This linear-time extraction capability highlights the profound synergy between flow algorithms and structural graph analysis. Rather than evaluating the exponential number 2^(V-2) of possible s-t cuts, the residual reachability traversal extracts the optimal bottleneck partition as an immediate, cost-free byproduct of the flow maximization phase. Whether isolating network partitions in distributed Raft clusters, analyzing attack surface perimeters in cyber-defense topologies, or segmenting objects in autonomous driving lidar point clouds, residual reachability provides an unbeatably fast, mathematically certified partitioning engine."
]

