p4_say = [
    "The deep mathematical duality between maximum flow and minimum cut manifests in bipartite graphs through a landmark theorem proven by Hungarian mathematician Denes Konig in 1931: Konig's Theorem. Konig's theorem establishes an exact equivalence between the size of a Maximum Matching and the size of a Minimum Vertex Cover in any bipartite graph. A Vertex Cover of a graph G = (V, E) is a subset of vertices C subseteq V such that every edge (u, v) in E has at least one endpoint in C (that is, u in C or v in C or both). A Minimum Vertex Cover is a vertex cover containing the smallest possible number of vertices. While computing the minimum vertex cover in general arbitrary graphs is famously NP-complete—representing one of Richard Karp's original 21 NP-complete problems—Konig's theorem proves that in bipartite graphs, the problem is solvable in polynomial time.",
    "The theorem states definitively: in any bipartite graph G = (U, W, E), the maximum number of edges in a matching equals the minimum number of vertices in a vertex cover: max |M| = min |C|. Intuitively, because each edge in a matching M shares no vertices with any other edge in M, any valid vertex cover must select at least one unique vertex to cover each matching edge. Therefore, the size of any vertex cover is bounded below by the size of any matching: |C| >= |M|. Konig's theorem proves the converse: there always exists a vertex cover whose size precisely equals the maximum matching size.",
    "The constructive proof of Konig's theorem directly utilizes the minimum cut extracted from the residual flow network. When maximum flow is computed on the bipartite matching network, the residual reachability traversal partitions the vertices into set S (reachable from source s) and set T (unreachable from s). Let L be the left partition U and R be the right partition W. The minimum vertex cover C is constructed by taking all left-side vertices that are unreachable from the source (L \\ S) combined with all right-side vertices that are reachable from the source (R cap S): C = (L \\ S) cup (R cap S).",
    "To verify that this set C is a valid vertex cover, consider any original edge (u, v) with u in L and v in R. If u is in L \\ S, it belongs to C, covering the edge. If u is in S, then vertex u is reachable from the source. Because edge (u, v) exists with capacity 1, if v were not in S, edge (u, v) would have residual capacity 0 (saturated with flow 1), meaning u in S and v in T forms part of the minimum cut. In the residual graph, flow on (u, v) creates a backward residual edge from v to u, but since u is reachable, v must also be reachable if flow is 0, or reachable via alternate paths. Detailed topological case analysis proves that no edge can have both u in S and v not in S without violating the residual reachability definition. Thus, every bipartite edge is covered.",
    "Furthermore, the capacity of the minimum cut in the flow network is precisely |L \\ S| + |R cap S|, because edges from source to L \\ S are saturated (capacity 1 each), and edges from R cap S to sink are saturated (capacity 1 each). Since minimum cut capacity equals maximum flow, and maximum flow equals maximum matching size, the size of C exactly equals the maximum matching size: |C| = |M*|. This constructive derivation bridges abstract graph theory and flow algorithms with absolute mathematical precision.",
    "In enterprise cybersecurity and access control governance, Konig's theorem provides the computational foundation for Least-Privilege Role Assignment and Asset Vulnerability Auditing. Consider a corporate security topology where left vertices represent employee roles and right vertices represent sensitive database servers, with edges representing access permissions. A minimum vertex cover identifies the absolute minimal set of roles and servers that must be monitored, audited, or equipped with multi-factor authentication (MFA) hardware tokens to guarantee 100% security coverage of all permission pathways. By applying Konig's theorem, enterprise security teams achieve complete compliance coverage with minimal administrative overhead."
]

